@page "/"

<PageTitle>FlociLab AWS</PageTitle>

<h1>FlociLab AWS</h1>

<p class="lede">
    AWS samples only — this app references <code>FlociLab.Core</code> and
    <code>FlociLab.Aws.S3.Demo</code> and nothing else, so it is the standalone artifact: clone
    <code>hosts/FlociLab.Aws.Web</code> plus the samples it references and you have an AWS demo
    with no other cloud SDK anywhere in the dependency tree.
</p>

<p>
    <a class="button" href="/coverage">Open the coverage matrix →</a>
</p>

<h2>The emulator</h2>

<table class="grid">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Cloud</th>
            <th>Image</th>
            <th>Endpoint</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>AWS</td><td><code>floci/floci</code></td><td><code>http://127.0.0.1:4566</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="note">
    The Floci web console on port 4500 lists AWS among the clouds it covers, but nothing this app
    starts brings it up — it comes with the Aspire AppHost, which runs
    <code>FlociLab.All.Web</code> rather than this host.
</p>

<p class="note">
    The endpoint comes from the <code>Floci:Aws</code> configuration section, and already defaults
    to the emulator's published port — so this app needs no configuration at all as long as
    <code>floci/floci</code> is running. The Aspire AppHost starts that container but does not run this
    host: it wires its live endpoints into <code>FlociLab.All.Web</code> only. Running inside the
    Compose network means setting <code>Floci__Aws__Endpoint=http://floci:4566</code>.
</p>
